imgsensor: honour the hardware privacy latch state at power-on

On crown and checkers the button that Android sees as the power key is
physically the privacy mute button. crown.dtsi wires it as:

    amazon-gating {
        state-gpios  = <&pio 48 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
        enable-gpios = <&pio 27 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
        input_event { label = "mute"; linux,code = <116>; gpios = <&pio 47>; };
    };

linux,code 116 is KEY_POWER, so a single long press does two unrelated
things at once: Android shows the power menu, and drivers/misc/gating.c
engages the hardware privacy latch that cuts camera power. The user sees
the red LED come on and has no reason to connect it to the camera.

That latch is hardware. It survives a reboot, and gating.c deliberately
refuses to release it from userspace ("Don't allow userspace to turn off
Privacy Mode"), so only another button press or removing mains power
clears it.

kdCISModulePowerOn() gates on cam_gate->req_state, but cam_gate is
kzalloc'd in kd_camera_init(), so req_state comes up as UNGATED, and it is
only ever updated by notifier callbacks - which cannot replay an event
from before the driver loaded. On any boot where the latch is engaged the
driver therefore believes it is ungated, raises cam_pwr_en, and runs the
whole power-on sequence into a rail the latch is holding down. Measured on
crown, with the gate pin and the latch sampled on-device at 100ms while
the kernel log ran:

    133.90 gate=hi latch=1
    141.87 [PowerON]pinSetIdx:0, currSensorName: ov9734_mipi_raw
    143.97 ISP_WaitIrq ERROR: IrqStatus(0x00000000),WaitStatus(0x00000400)
    145.97 [PowerON]pinSetIdx:0, currSensorName: ov9734_mipi_raw
    148.04 i2c-mt65xx 11007000.i2c: addr: 36, transfer timeout

The sensor is powered on paper and dead in fact: no MIPI frame ever
arrives, and after the driver's retry power-cycle it stops acknowledging
on i2c altogether. Every reboot rebuilds the same wrong assumption, which
is why users report that nothing short of unplugging the device for ~20s
brings the camera back - the unplug is not resetting the sensor, it is
resetting the latch. Reported as issue #4.

This adds gating_current_state() to gating.c so the latch can be read
directly rather than inferred, and syncs req_state from it in
set_gate_state() before the UNGATED check.

The sync has to run in both directions. The latch releases on any button
press, but gating.c only notifies for presses it treats as long (>=500ms),
so a short press clears the latch in hardware and tells nobody. An earlier
version of this patch synced only the GATED direction; on device that left
req_state stuck at GATED after the user had already cleared the shutter,
and the camera stayed refused until a reboot. Reading the hardware in both
directions also makes the notifier a latency optimisation rather than the
source of truth, which is what the reboot case needs anyway.

Verified on crown:

  - latch clear: camera opens normally, no change in behaviour
  - latch engaged, then rebooted: power-on is refused at once with
    "cannot UNGATE CAM", and no [PowerON]/ISP timeout/i2c wedge follows
  - latch then cleared with a short press: camera works again with no
    reboot and no unplug

This is not sufficient on its own when the latch is engaged across a
reboot. cameraserver runs its sensor search once at boot, finds nothing
while the latch is engaged, and caches an empty device list for the life
of the process, so clearing the latch afterwards changes nothing until
something restarts it. Patch 0016 closes that, and depends on the
gating_current_state() added here.

Apply from the kernel tree root (kernel/amazon/mt8163-4.9):
    patch -p1 < 0015-camera-honour-the-privacy-latch-state-at-power-on.patch

diff --git a/drivers/misc/gating.c b/drivers/misc/gating.c
index 1624ae0a..bcbb728c 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/gating.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/gating.c
@@ -53,6 +53,7 @@ struct gating_priv {
 };
 
 static struct srcu_notifier_head *priv_nh;
+static struct gating_priv *gating_priv_ref;
 
 int register_gating_state_notifier(struct notifier_block *nb)
 {
@@ -79,6 +80,24 @@ static enum gating_state __gating_state(struct gating_priv *priv)
 	return value;
 }
 
+/* gating_current_state() : read the privacy latch as it is right now.
+ *
+ * The latch is hardware and survives a reboot, but drivers that care about
+ * it only learn of changes through the notifier chain, which by definition
+ * cannot replay events from before they were loaded. Without this they come
+ * up assuming UNGATED and act on that assumption for the whole boot.
+ *
+ * Returns UNGATED/GATED, or -ENODEV before the gating driver has probed.
+ */
+int gating_current_state(void)
+{
+	if (!gating_priv_ref)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	return __gating_state(gating_priv_ref);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(gating_current_state);
+
 static int __set_gating_state(struct gating_priv *priv, int enable)
 {
 	int i = 0;
@@ -490,6 +509,11 @@ static int gating_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		device_init_wakeup(&pdev->dev,
 					priv->input_event->wakeup_capable);
 
+	/* Published last, so gating_current_state() never sees a half-set-up
+	 * priv. Everything it touches is initialised by this point.
+	 */
+	gating_priv_ref = priv;
+
 	dev_info(dev, "%s : done\n", __func__);
 
 	return 0;
@@ -504,6 +528,8 @@ static int gating_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	priv = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
 	priv_event = priv->input_event;
 
+	gating_priv_ref = NULL;
+
 	devm_free_irq(dev,
 		      gpiod_to_irq(priv->state_gpio),
 		      "gating");
diff --git a/drivers/misc/mediatek/imgsensor/src/mt8163/camera_hw/camera_project/checkers/kd_camera_hw.c b/drivers/misc/mediatek/imgsensor/src/mt8163/camera_hw/camera_project/checkers/kd_camera_hw.c
index cecaaf70..0abed185 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/mediatek/imgsensor/src/mt8163/camera_hw/camera_project/checkers/kd_camera_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/mediatek/imgsensor/src/mt8163/camera_hw/camera_project/checkers/kd_camera_hw.c
@@ -81,11 +81,33 @@ static enum gating_state conf_cam_gate(enum gating_state state)
 /* set_gate_state() : check and configure the camera gating state */
 static enum gating_state set_gate_state(enum gating_state state)
 {
+	int hw_state;
+
 	if (!cam_gate)
 		return state;
 
 	mutex_lock(&cam_gate->mutex);
 
+	/* req_state only ever tracks notifier callbacks, and cam_gate is
+	 * kzalloc'd at probe, so after a reboot it reads UNGATED no matter
+	 * what the privacy latch is actually doing. The latch is hardware and
+	 * survives the reboot, so believing req_state here means powering the
+	 * sensor into a rail the latch is holding down: the driver logs
+	 * [PowerON], no MIPI frame ever arrives (ISP_WaitIrq IrqStatus 0) and
+	 * the sensor then drops off i2c entirely. Ask the gating driver what
+	 * the latch is really doing before trusting the cached value.
+	 *
+	 * Sync both ways. The latch releases on any button press, but gating.c
+	 * only raises a notification for presses it considers long (>=500ms),
+	 * so a short press clears the latch in hardware without telling anyone.
+	 * Syncing only the GATED direction would then leave req_state stuck at
+	 * GATED with no path back, and the camera would stay refused after the
+	 * user had already cleared the shutter.
+	 */
+	hw_state = gating_current_state();
+	if (hw_state == UNGATED || hw_state == GATED)
+		cam_gate->req_state = hw_state;
+
 	/* Make sure device is in UNGATED Mode before trying to enable CAM */
 	if (state == UNGATED && cam_gate->req_state != UNGATED) {
 		pr_info("Failed to enable CAM, GATING Mode is ON\n");
@@ -387,7 +409,7 @@ int kdCISModulePowerOn(enum CAMERA_DUAL_CAMERA_SENSOR_ENUM SensorIdx, char *curr
 	if (On) {
 		/* Check cam_gate state before powering on sensor */
 		if (set_gate_state(UNGATED) != UNGATED) {
-			PK_DBG("[CAMERA SENSOR] Falied to UNGATE CAM\n");
+			PK_ERR("[CAMERA SENSOR] cannot UNGATE CAM: the privacy latch is engaged; press the mute button to clear it\n");
 			goto _kdCISModulePowerOn_exit_;
 		}
 
